FINAL ANSWER:
**Short answer:** Lena Brandt and Marco Silva are the only people who can review these areas today. The top committer, dblock, doesn't own the most code.

I ran the busfactor script with `--no-github`, so people are identified by git email, not GitHub handle. Marco appears under two emails (`marco.silva@kestrel.example` and `<email redacted>`). I merged them by hand and assumed they're the same person.

## Who to route to

| Area | Best reviewers | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `lib/grape_entity/exposure/` | **Lena Brandt**, then **Anna Kowalczyk** and **Marco Silva** | Each has made a single commit here (Jul–Aug 2026), and Marco's was the laptop email. |
| Delegator code (`delegator.rb` and `delegators/`) | **Marco Silva**, then **Lena Brandt** | Marco made the only commit in `delegators/` (2026-07-21). |

All three are recent but thin. Between them they have 1–2 commits in these areas. They are the only active reviewers, not deep experts.

The script flags both delegators and `spec/grape_entity/exposure/` as bus factor 1. In practice, Marco and Lena cover each other, so a single absence stalls reviews rather than leaving nobody who can approve.

## Who has effectively left

The repo history runs to 2026-10-05, and I treated anyone with no commit since about October 2025 as gone. Most of the historical authors fall in that group.

- **Vladimir Kochnev** (`<email redacted>`) wrote the original exposure and delegator code in 2015. His last commit is 2015-09-18. He still has about 756 surviving lines in `lib/`, by far the most.
- **Peter Scholz / LeFnord** (`<email redacted>`) was the longest-running exposure contributor. His last commit is 2020-12-26.
- **Dmitry Tsepelev** (2018), **Alexander Yunin** (2016), **James McCarthy** (2017) and **Mikael Henriksson** (2019) are also gone.
- The other past contributors to these areas, from 2016–2023, have likewise been inactive for years.

Don't request reviews from any of these people. GitHub would block PRs on someone who can't respond.

## Does the top committer own the most code?

No, on either measure.

- **Most commits:** dblock, with 38 under that email and about 60 across his aliases. Most of that is maintenance and releases.
- **Most surviving code in `lib/`:** Vladimir Kochnev (about 756 lines), who has been gone since 2015. Next are Mark Madsen (237) and Marco Silva (143, laptop email). dblock has only about 34.
- **Active ownership:** Marco and Lena are the only people active in the last 12 months.

So the most-committing author owns little of the live code, and the person with the most surviving code left over a decade ago.

## Caveats

- **Imported history:** The script reports the repo as imported, with a bulk first commit of 18 files in 2013. Early ownership is therefore unreliable.
- **Commits are a proxy:** Commit counts don't measure who actually understands the code.
- **No files written:** I used `--dry-run`, so no CODEOWNERS file was written.

I can write `.github/CODEOWNERS` and the ownership JSON if you want, or build the HTML risk report.

